<TopicPage slug="subjects/astronomy/expert/cosmology-and-general-relativity">
  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This lesson generalises ideas you have already used on smaller stages. <em>Galaxies and
    the Expanding Universe</em> (advanced) introduced recession and Hubble's law for nearby
    galaxies; <em>Compact Objects</em> (advanced) showed general relativity operating around
    single masses; <em>Stellar Life Cycles</em> (intermediate) supplied the stellar physics
    and ages we will check the cosmic clock against. Now the same machinery runs on the
    universe whole.
  </p>
  <p>By the end you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>compute a cosmological redshift from emitted and observed wavelengths and explain why it is space stretching, not Doppler recession;</li>
    <li>show how the model forces an age of 13.8 billion years and an observable universe 93 billion light-years across — and why those numbers do not contradict;</li>
    <li>rehearse the three independent evidence streams for the hot Big Bang and name the physics domain each draws on;</li>
    <li>reason from the universe's composition to its fate, and say what the parameter w measures;</li>
    <li>state the horizon and flatness problems and explain what inflation claims to solve.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Cosmology applies general relativity to the universe whole: its geometry, its history,
    its fate. The modern model (ΛCDM) fits an extraordinary range of data with a handful of
    numbers: an expanding universe, 13.8 billion years old, made of ~5% ordinary matter,
    ~27% dark matter and ~68% dark energy. Each of those percentages is measured, not
    assumed — and two of them are, embarrassing and wonderful, unexplained. This page
    builds the model from the syllabus's four stages: the recession of the galaxies and
    the law behind it, the age and size those measurements force, the three independent
    evidence streams for the hot beginning, and the dark components that decide the end.
    The expert habit throughout: every claim is a fit to data, and the residuals are
    where the live research lives.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the Big Bang case: three independent evidence streams and one outstanding theory" widget="equationStep" config={cosmoCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> the cards are ordered as evidence first, theory second.
    Cards 2 to 4 are the three independent streams — expansion, afterglow, abundances — each
    resting on different physics, which is why the case is strong: they fail separately or
    not at all. Card 1 is the claim those streams force, and card 5 is deliberately flagged
    as still under test. Watch where the reasoning switches from measurement to hypothesis,
    and keep that boundary in view for the rest of the lesson.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> cosmology is the attempt to fit one model to everything the sky
    shows, and its residuals are where the live research lives. The first step is the
    foundation measurement itself: how we know the galaxies recede, and what the redshift
    actually measures.
  </p>

  <h2>Recession, redshift and the distance–redshift relationship</h2>
  <p>
    The foundation measurement: light from distant galaxies is shifted toward the red, and
    in 1929 Edwin Hubble showed the shift grows with distance — v = H₀d, with H₀ the
    Hubble constant. The mechanism matters: this is not galaxies flying through space but
    <em>space itself stretching</em>, doubling the wavelength of light as it doubles the
    distance — cosmological redshift, technically z = (λ_observed − λ_emitted)/λ_emitted.
    Worked example: the Lyman-α line is emitted at 121.6 nm; observed at 486 nm, the
    redshift is z = 486/121.6 − 1 ≈ 3.0, and the universe has stretched by a factor of
    1 + z = 4 since that light left (the scale factor was a quarter of today's). Hubble's
    law with H₀ ≈ 70 km/s per megaparsec puts a galaxy at 100 Mpc receding at ~7,000
    km/s. Two refinements the expert must carry: the law holds only at modest distances
    (beyond a few billion light-years the expansion's history — acceleration — bends the
    relation, which is exactly how dark energy was found), and Hubble's original
    distance calibration was wrong by a factor of about seven (Cepheid calibration errors),
    corrected by decades of distance-ladder work. The constant is now measured two
    independent ways that disagree — the Hubble tension, treated below.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "the cosmological redshift is a Doppler shift from
    galaxies speeding away." Not quite: Doppler is motion <em>through</em> space;
    cosmological redshift is the stretching of light's wavelength <em>by the expansion
    of space</em> during transit. For nearby galaxies the two descriptions agree; for
    distant ones they diverge — the most distant observed galaxies have redshifts above
    14 (JWST era), corresponding to recession "speeds" that exceed c, which no Doppler
    picture permits and no relativity law forbids, because nothing is moving through
    space faster than light.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> the expansion is measured, and the redshift is the stretching of
    light by space itself — a quantity that converts directly into stretch factor. Run the
    film backward and the model must commit to numbers: an age, a size, and a geometry.
  </p>

  <h2>Age and size: the arithmetic the model forces</h2>
  <p>
    Run the expansion backward: a universe now expanding at rate H₀ was denser and hotter
    in the past, and the film's first frame is the Big Bang. The age falls out of the
    expansion history: for the measured H₀ and matter/energy contents, general
    relativity's Friedmann equations give 13.8 billion years — checked independently
    against the oldest globular-cluster stars (about 13 billion, with the earliest
    generations forming right after) and the radioactive dating of ancient stars
    (uranium-238 and thorium-232 chronometry). The size question needs care: the
    <em>observable</em> universe spans about 93 billion light-years across, much larger
    than 13.8 billion light-years, because the space that emitted the light we receive
    today has itself stretched while the light travelled — the edge of visibility, not a
    wall. The geometry behind the arithmetic is measured, not assumed: the total
    energy density (from the cosmic microwave background's acoustic peak structure)
    comes out equal to the critical density to within about half a percent — a
    <em>flat</em> universe, which is itself one of cosmology's strangest and most
    informative facts (why so flat? inflation, below).
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "the universe is 13.8 billion years old, so the
    farthest visible objects are 13.8 billion light-years away." The light took 13.8
    billion years to arrive, but the <em>emitting region</em> has been receding and
    stretching all the while: the matter that emitted the CMB we see today is now about
    46 billion light-years away, and the observable universe is about 93 billion
    light-years across. Distance "then", distance "now" and light-travel time are three
    different quantities in an expanding universe — conflating them is the most common
    size-of-the-universe error in popular writing.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> the arithmetic is fixed by measurement, not assumption — age from
    the expansion history, cross-checked against the oldest stars; size from following the
    stretching; geometry from the CMB's acoustic peaks. A model that commits to a hot, dense
    beginning now owes an explanation of what that beginning would leave behind. It leaves
    three things, on three independent physics.
  </p>

  <h2>Evidence for the Big Bang: three independent streams</h2>
  <p>
    The hot Big Bang is not one observation but three, on independent physics.
    <strong>Stream one: the expansion itself</strong> (above) — running it backward
    demands a hot dense beginning. <strong>Stream two: the cosmic microwave
    background</strong> — the universe's oldest light, released at 380,000 years when
    the plasma cooled enough for neutral atoms and photons to stream free, now cooled by
    expansion to 2.7255 K and filling every direction of the sky. Predicted by
    Alpher and Herman in 1948 from nucleosynthesis physics, found accidentally by
    Penzias and Wilson in 1965 (they spent months removing "antenna noise" before
    accepting the sky itself was glowing), mapped by COBE, WMAP and Planck to a uniformity
    of one part in 100,000 — with the fluctuations preserved: the seeds of every galaxy.
    <strong>Stream three: element abundances</strong> — Big Bang nucleosynthesis in the
    first three minutes predicts roughly 75% hydrogen and 25% helium by mass (plus traces
    of deuterium and lithium), fixed by the baryon density measured independently from
    the CMB; the measured primordial helium fraction matches. Three physics domains —
    galaxy dynamics, photon gas thermodynamics, nuclear reaction networks — converge on
    the same story. That is why the Big Bang is consensus and not narrative.
  </p>
  <p>
    The CMB carries two further quantitative prizes. Its <strong>dipole</strong> —
    a part-in-a-thousand warm/cool split across the sky — is our own motion at 370
    km/s against the cosmic rest frame, the Solar System's speedometer written in
    light. And the spectrum is a near-perfect black body, the most precise such
    measurement made in nature — exactly what an early plasma in thermal
    equilibrium must leave behind, and something no later galaxy-scale process
    could fake. The fine structure — the fluctuations at one part in 100,000 —
    encodes the acoustic physics of that primordial plasma, and reading them is
    where the model's six parameters come from.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "the Big Bang was an explosion at a point in
    pre-existing space." The model says space itself began (or at least began expanding):
    every point was the hot dense state, and the CMB arrives from every direction because
    it was emitted everywhere at once. There is no centre to locate and no edge to
    approach; asking "where did it happen?" is like asking where on Earth's surface is
    the centre of the globe. "Big Bang" was Fred Hoyle's radio-broadcast jibe (1949);
    the name stuck because the theory did.
  </p>

  <h2>Dark matter, dark energy and the fate of the Universe</h2>
  <p>
    The inventory completes with the two darks (the next page treats their evidence in
    depth). <strong>Dark matter</strong> (~27%): unseen mass whose gravity shapes
    galaxies, clusters and the CMB's acoustic peaks — required, not optional, in any
    model fitting the data. <strong>Dark energy</strong> (~68%): the component whose
    constant density makes expansion accelerate — discovered 1998 when distant Type Ia
    supernovae came out fainter than a decelerating universe would allow. In ΛCDM the
    future follows from the composition: with dark energy constant, expansion continues
    to accelerate; galaxies beyond our local group recede past the cosmic horizon;
    star formation exhausts gas; and the universe fades toward a cold dilute equilibrium
    — the "heat death", the current best estimate. Alternative endings depend on dark
    energy's nature: a strengthening component tears galaxies, stars and atoms apart
    (Big Rip); a decaying one lets the universe recollapse. The Friedmann equations
    make the question quantitative: measure w — the pressure-to-density ratio of the
    dark component — and the fate follows. Current data: w ≈ −1, consistent with a
    constant, with tentative DESI hints (2024–25) that it may evolve.
  </p>

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. Feedback follows each answer.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Name the three independent evidence streams for the hot Big Bang and the physics domain each one draws on.</li>
    <li>A quasar's Lyman-alpha line, emitted at 121.6 nm, arrives at 486 nm. Compute the redshift z and state by what factor the universe has stretched since that light left.</li>
    <li>Why can the observable universe span about 93 billion light-years even though the universe is only 13.8 billion years old?</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Feedback:</strong> (1) The expansion itself (galaxy dynamics and the distance–
    redshift relation); the cosmic microwave background (photon gas thermodynamics — a
    2.7 K black body predicted in 1948 and found in 1965); and element abundances (nuclear
    reaction networks fixing roughly 75% hydrogen and 25% helium in the first three
    minutes). Three domains, one story — that convergence is what makes the Big Bang
    consensus rather than narrative. (2) z = 486/121.6 − 1 ≈ 3.0, so the stretch factor is
    1 + z = 4: the scale factor was a quarter of today's when the light left. If you skipped
    the "minus one", you computed a stretch factor where the definition asks for the fractional
    change. (3) Because distance "then", distance "now" and light-travel time are three
    different quantities in an expanding universe. The light travelled for 13.8 billion years,
    but the emitting region has receded and stretched all the while — the matter that emitted
    today's CMB is now about 46 billion light-years away, so the observable universe's span
    exceeds the age times the speed of light with no relativity violation.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Cosmological redshift is the stretching of light's wavelength by expanding space — not a Doppler shift through space.</li>
    <li>Hubble's law holds at modest distances; at large distances the expansion's history bends the relation, which is exactly how dark energy was found.</li>
    <li>The model forces an age of 13.8 billion years and an observable universe about 93 billion light-years across; geometry is measured flat to about half a percent.</li>
    <li>Three independent evidence streams — expansion, CMB, abundances — converge on the hot Big Bang.</li>
    <li>The CMB is a near-perfect black body with fluctuations at one part in 100,000; the model's six parameters are read from those fluctuations.</li>
    <li>Composition decides fate: with dark energy constant (w ≈ −1), expansion accelerates toward heat death; a strengthening or decaying component changes the ending.</li>
    <li>Inflation answers the horizon and flatness problems with one mechanism; the Hubble tension is today's most-cited unresolved residual.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    The two darks get their own expert page: <em>Dark Matter and Dark Energy</em> treats the
    evidence this lesson could only inventory, rotation curves to supernovae. <em>Astrobiology</em>
    then asks what the model's timeline means for habitable windows — the same universe,
    13.8 billion years old, now hosting at least one biosphere to explain.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Inflation and the horizon problem.</strong> The deepest puzzle of the
    standard model: opposite sides of the sky — never in causal contact, since light
    hasn't had time to pass between them — share the same temperature to one part in
    100,000, and the universe's geometry is measured flat to half a percent. Inflation
    (Guth 1981; Linde, Albrecht–Steinhardt 1982) answers both with one mechanism: an
    epoch of exponential expansion around 10⁻³⁶ to 10⁻³² seconds, stretching a
    causally-connected patch to cosmic size and flattening the geometry like the skin
    of an inflating balloon. Its bonus prediction is cosmology's crown jewel: quantum
    fluctuations, stretched to cosmic scale, seed the CMB's fluctuations with a
    nearly-scale-invariant spectrum — measured by Planck to few-percent agreement.
    Open frontier: primordial gravitational waves (inflation's smoking gun, hunted in
    the CMB's polarisation — the 2014 BICEP2 claim was dust, not cosmology), and the
    theory's own embarrassment of models — inflation explains so much that it predicts
    too much, and distinguishing variants is the active business.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>The Hubble tension, precisely.</strong> The early-universe route: fit
    ΛCDM to the Planck CMB and the model <em>predicts</em> H₀ ≈ 67.4 km/s/Mpc. The
    late-universe route: build the distance ladder (Cepheids, then Type Ia supernovae)
    and measure H₀ ≈ 73 km/s/Mpc — the SH0ES programme's result, now refined by
    multiple independent ladders (TRGB stars, masers, lensed quasars) clustering high.
    The disagreement is around 8–9% — five standard deviations and growing, far beyond
    either method's stated errors. Possibilities: unknown systematic in one of the
    ladders (the conservative bet), or genuine new physics in ΛCDM (early dark energy,
    extra neutrino species — models exist, none clean). Either resolution is a
    discovery: this is the field's most cited "small number problem", and a textbook
    example of how a mature model meets its eventual successor — through a residual
    nobody could dismiss.
  </p>
  <p>
    The distance ladder itself is worth understanding, because every cosmological
    number stands on it. Rung one is geometric: parallax (Gaia measures stellar
    distances to microarcsecond precision across the galaxy). Rung two is standard
    candles: Cepheid variables, whose pulse period reveals their luminosity
    (Leavitt's law), calibrate supernova host galaxies; Type Ia supernovae, whose
    light curves self-calibrate, extend to cosmological distances. Rung three is
    the standard <em>ruler</em>: baryon acoustic oscillations — the sound horizon
    of the primordial plasma, frozen at about 490 million light-years into the
    distribution of galaxies, measured by surveys of millions of them. Each rung
    transfers calibration to the next, and the ladder's overlapping checks
    (masers, lensed quasars, tip-of-the-red-giant-branch stars) are precisely how
    the Hubble tension was cornered: too many independent methods now agree with
    each other while disagreeing with the early-universe route.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>ΛCDM's remaining cracks.</strong> Beyond the tension: the S₈ tension
    (clustering amplitude measured by weak lensing runs slightly low versus the CMB
    prediction); the "impossibly early galaxies" JWST is finding (massive, mature
    galaxies at redshift above 10, stress-testing structure formation's timeline);
    the missing-satellites and too-big-to-fail problems of galaxy formation
    (dark-matter simulations predict dwarf galaxies not observed — possibly
    astrophysics, possibly the dark sector); and the DESI collaboration's
    2024–25 hints that dark energy's density may decrease with time — tentative
    (two-to-four sigma depending on dataset combinations), contested, and the first
    serious crack in the cosmological constant's forty-year reign. The honest posture:
    ΛCDM fits thousands of data points with six parameters — no model in science
    does more with less — and every one of these tensions is the next decade's
    experiment queue. The model will not be overthrown by philosophy; it will be
    overthrown by a residual.
  </p>
